<text top="96" left="512" width="7" height="14" font="3">3</text>
<text top="137" left="55" width="218" height="17" font="5">G.7 File System Defragmenter</text>
<text top="166" left="105" width="414" height="14" font="3">This project explores on-disk data structures and their performance</text>
<text top="181" left="87" width="432" height="14" font="3">implications. The project should give some particular file-system images</text>
<text top="196" left="87" width="432" height="14" font="3">to students with known fragmentation problems; students should then</text>
<text top="211" left="87" width="432" height="14" font="3">crawl through the image, and look for files that are not laid out sequen-</text>
<text top="226" left="87" width="432" height="14" font="3">tially. Write out a new “defragmented” image that fixes this problem,</text>
<text top="241" left="87" width="199" height="14" font="3">perhaps reporting some statistics.</text>
<text top="278" left="55" width="194" height="17" font="5">G.8 Concurrent File Server</text>
<text top="308" left="105" width="414" height="14" font="3">This project combines concurrency and file systems and even a little</text>
<text top="323" left="87" width="432" height="14" font="3">bit of networking and distributed systems. Students build a simple con-</text>
<text top="338" left="87" width="432" height="14" font="3">current file server. The protocol should look something like NFS, with</text>
<text top="353" left="87" width="432" height="14" font="3">lookups, reads, writes, and stats. Store files within a single disk image</text>
<text top="368" left="87" width="432" height="14" font="3">(designed as a file). Variants are manifold, with different suggested on-</text>
<text top="382" left="87" width="216" height="14" font="3">disk formats and network protocols.</text>
